(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("k8s-lifecycle",{"k8s-lifecycle/designing-a-highly-available-control-plane":{"html":"\u003cp>A single control plane node is a single point of failure for every change in the cluster. High availability means running several control plane nodes so that the loss of one does not stop operations. The design choices are few but each has consequences for cost, failure tolerance and recovery.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Stacked and external etcd topologies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why three control plane nodes is the common minimum\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The load balancer and the control plane endpoint\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Failure domains and zone placement\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What high availability does not protect\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stacked-or-external-etcd\">Stacked or external etcd\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In a stacked topology each control plane node runs etcd as a static pod next to the API server, scheduler and controller manager. It needs fewer machines and is simple to operate with kubeadm. The cost is coupling: losing a node loses an API server and an etcd member together. In an external topology etcd runs on its own nodes. It separates failure domains and lets you size and secure etcd independently, at the price of more machines to manage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-three\">Why three\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Raft needs a majority, so one member gives no tolerance, two gives none either, and three tolerates one failure. Three control plane nodes is therefore the usual minimum for production. Five tolerates two failures and suits very large or critical clusters, but adds write latency because more members must acknowledge.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-control-plane-endpoint\">The control plane endpoint\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Nodes and users must reach any healthy API server through one stable address. A load balancer or a virtual IP in front of the API servers provides it, and it is given to kubeadm as the control plane endpoint. Health checks should test the API server port or its /readyz, and the load balancer itself must not become a single point of failure. Kubeconfig files and kubelets use this endpoint, so it is hard to change later.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"failure-domains\">Failure domains\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Spread control plane nodes across failure domains such as availability zones or racks, so that one zone failing leaves a majority. With three nodes put one in each of three zones. Two zones are worse than they look, because losing the zone that holds two members loses quorum. Keep network latency between members low, since etcd commits depend on it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-ha-does-not-cover\">What HA does not cover\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Several control plane nodes do not protect against a bad upgrade applied to all of them, a mistaken delete, corrupted data replicated to every member, or loss of the whole site. Those need tested backups and a recovery plan, covered later in this module. Workers are separate: run enough of them in several zones.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubeadm init --control-plane-endpoint &quot;k8s-api.example.internal:6443&quot; --upload-certs --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubeadm init --control-plane-endpoint \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;k8s-api.example.internal:6443&quot;\u003c/span> --upload-certs --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"lb&gt;c1;lb&gt;c2;lb&gt;c3;c2&gt;w\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Designing a highly available control plane\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lb\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Load balancer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"c1\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Control plane A\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"c2\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Control plane B\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"c3\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Control plane C\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"w\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Workers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Load balancer to Control plane A; 2, Load balancer to Control plane B; 3, Load balancer to Control plane C; 4, Control plane B to Workers.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lb\">\u003cdt>Load balancer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One stable API endpoint.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Single instance.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"c1\">\u003cdt>Control plane A \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> API, etcd member.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Zone outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"c2\">\u003cdt>Control plane B \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> API, etcd member.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Zone outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"c3\">\u003cdt>Control plane C \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> API, etcd member.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Zone outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"w\">\u003cdt>Workers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run workloads.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too few zones.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-zones-one-of-them-lost\">Incident: Two zones, one of them lost\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cluster with three control plane nodes in two zones lost the zone that held two of them.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get nodes\netcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://10.0.1.5:2379\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Unable to connect to the server: net/http: TLS handshake timeout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>etcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://10.0.1.5:2379\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">https://10.0.1.5:2379 is unhealthy: failed to commit proposal: context deadline exceeded\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Two of three etcd members were in the zone that failed, so quorum was lost and the remaining API server could not serve requests. Placing one node in each of three zones would have left a majority. The team restored quorum by recovering a lost member, then moved a node to a third zone. Count members per failure domain, not only nodes in total.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using two control plane nodes and calling it highly available\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting two members in the same failure domain of three\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a single load balancer instance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing the control plane endpoint casually\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating HA as a replacement for backups\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"stacked-or-external-etcd","text":"Stacked or external etcd"},{"id":"why-three","text":"Why three"},{"id":"the-control-plane-endpoint","text":"The control plane endpoint"},{"id":"failure-domains","text":"Failure domains"},{"id":"what-ha-does-not-cover","text":"What HA does not cover"},{"id":"incident-two-zones-one-of-them-lost","text":"Incident: Two zones, one of them lost"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Options for highly available topology","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/setup/production-environment/tools/kubeadm/ha-topology/"},{"t":"Creating highly available clusters with kubeadm","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/setup/production-environment/tools/kubeadm/high-availability/"}],"text":"A single control plane node is a single point of failure for every change in the cluster. High availability means running several control plane nodes so that the loss of one does not stop operations. The design choices are few but each has consequences for cost, failure tolerance and recovery. What you will learn Stacked and external etcd topologies Why three control plane nodes is the common minimum The load balancer and the control plane endpoint Failure domains and zone placement What high availability does not protect Stacked or external etcd In a stacked topology each control plane node runs etcd as a static pod next to the API server, scheduler and controller manager. It needs fewer machines and is simple to operate with kubeadm. The cost is coupling: losing a node loses an API server and an etcd member together. In an external topology etcd runs on its own nodes. It separates failure domains and lets you size and secure etcd independently, at the price of more machines to manage. Why three Raft needs a majority, so one member gives no tolerance, two gives none either, and three tolerates one failure. Three control plane nodes is therefore the usual minimum for production. Five tolerates two failures and suits very large or critical clusters, but adds write latency because more members must acknowledge. The control plane endpoint Nodes and users must reach any healthy API server through one stable address. A load balancer or a virtual IP in front of the API servers provides it, and it is given to kubeadm as the control plane endpoint. Health checks should test the API server port or its /readyz, and the load balancer itself must not become a single point of failure. Kubeconfig files and kubelets use this endpoint, so it is hard to change later. Failure domains Spread control plane nodes across failure domains such as availability zones or racks, so that one zone failing leaves a majority. With three nodes put one in each of three zones. Two zones are worse than they look, because losing the zone that holds two members loses quorum. Keep network latency between members low, since etcd commits depend on it. What HA does not cover Several control plane nodes do not protect against a bad upgrade applied to all of them, a mistaken delete, corrupted data replicated to every member, or loss of the whole site. Those need tested backups and a recovery plan, covered later in this module. Workers are separate: run enough of them in several zones. kubeadm init --control-plane-endpoint \"k8s-api.example.internal:6443\" --upload-certs --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 Designing a highly available control plane Flow in order: 1, Load balancer to Control plane A; 2, Load balancer to Control plane B; 3, Load balancer to Control plane C; 4, Control plane B to Workers. Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Load balancer Service What it does: One stable API endpoint. If it fails or is weak: Single instance. Control plane A Service What it does: API, etcd member. If it fails or is weak: Zone outage. Control plane B Service What it does: API, etcd member. If it fails or is weak: Zone outage. Control plane C Service What it does: API, etcd member. If it fails or is weak: Zone outage. Workers Step What it does: Run workloads. If it fails or is weak: Too few zones. Incident: Two zones, one of them lost Incident A cluster with three control plane nodes in two zones lost the zone that held two of them. $ kubectl get nodes Unable to connect to the server: net/http: TLS handshake timeout $ etcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://10.0.1.5:2379 https://10.0.1.5:2379 is unhealthy: failed to commit proposal: context deadline exceeded (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Two of three etcd members were in the zone that failed, so quorum was lost and the remaining API server could not serve requests. Placing one node in each of three zones would hav"},"k8s-lifecycle/bootstrapping-with-kubeadm-configuration-and-phases":{"html":"\u003cp>kubeadm sets up the pieces of a cluster in a defined order and leaves the nodes in a known state. Using a configuration file and understanding the phases means a cluster can be rebuilt the same way each time and a failed bootstrap can be repaired instead of restarted blindly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Prerequisites on every node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The main phases of kubeadm init\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A configuration file instead of flags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Joining control plane and worker nodes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resetting and retrying safely\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"prerequisites\">Prerequisites\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every node needs a container runtime with the CRI enabled, the kubelet, kubeadm and kubectl packages at matching versions, swap disabled unless configured for it, required kernel modules and sysctls for bridging and IP forwarding, unique host names, and open ports between nodes. Most bootstrap failures are a missed prerequisite, and \u003ccode>kubeadm init\u003c/code> runs preflight checks that name them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"phases-of-init\">Phases of init\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>kubeadm init\u003c/code> runs phases in order: preflight, certificate generation, kubeconfig files, kubelet start, control plane static pod manifests, etcd static pod, wait for the control plane, upload configuration, mark the control plane node, bootstrap tokens and add-ons such as CoreDNS and kube-proxy. You can list and run a single phase with \u003ccode>kubeadm init phase\u003c/code>, which is useful for repairs, for example regenerating a certificate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-configuration-file\">A configuration file\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Flags are fine for a lab. For anything repeatable, use a configuration file with the InitConfiguration and ClusterConfiguration kinds. It records the Kubernetes version, the control plane endpoint, the pod and service subnets, certificate SANs and component flags.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: kubeadm.k8s.io/v1beta4\nkind: ClusterConfiguration\nkubernetesVersion: v1.31.2\ncontrolPlaneEndpoint: k8s-api.example.internal:6443\nnetworking:\n  podSubnet: 10.244.0.0/16\n  serviceSubnet: 10.96.0.0/12\napiServer:\n  certSANs:\n    - k8s-api.example.internal\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">kubeadm.k8s.io/v1beta4\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ClusterConfiguration\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kubernetesVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1.31.2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">controlPlaneEndpoint:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">k8s-api.example.internal:6443\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">networking:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">podSubnet:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.244\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/16\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">serviceSubnet:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10.96\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/12\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiServer:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">certSANs:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">k8s-api.example.internal\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubeadm init --config kubeadm.yaml --upload-certs\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubeadm init --config kubeadm.yaml --upload-certs\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"joining-nodes\">Joining nodes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Workers join with a token and the CA hash printed by init. Tokens expire after 24 hours by default, so create a new one with \u003ccode>kubeadm token create --print-join-command\u003c/code>. Additional control plane nodes join with the control plane flag and a certificate key, which is valid for two hours. After joining, install a network plugin if you have not, then check that all nodes are Ready.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reset-and-retry\">Reset and retry\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A failed init or join leaves files behind. \u003ccode>kubeadm reset\u003c/code> removes what kubeadm created on that node, but not CNI configuration, iptables rules or your kubeconfig. Clean those by hand before retrying. Read the preflight and phase output first, because the line that failed is usually clear.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"pre&gt;pki;pki&gt;cp;cp&gt;add;add&gt;join\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Bootstrapping with kubeadm: configuration and phases\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pre\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Preflight\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pki\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Certificates\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cp\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Control plane\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"add\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Add-ons\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"join\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Join nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Preflight to Certificates; 2, Certificates to Control plane; 3, Control plane to Add-ons; 4, Add-ons to Join nodes.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pre\">\u003cdt>Preflight \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Checks the node.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Swap or ports.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pki\">\u003cdt>Certificates \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates the CA and certs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong SANs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cp\">\u003cdt>Control plane \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Static pod manifests.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bad flags.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"add\">\u003cdt>Add-ons \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> CoreDNS and kube-proxy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No CNI yet.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"join\">\u003cdt>Join nodes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Token and CA hash.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Expired token.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-join-token-that-expired\">Incident: The join token that expired\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new worker could not join a cluster built the week before.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo kubeadm join k8s-api.example.internal:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1f2e3d\nkubeadm token list\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">join\u003c/span> k8s-api.example.internal:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1f2e3d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error execution phase preflight: couldn't validate the identity of the API Server: could not find a JWS signature in the cluster-info ConfigMap for token ID &quot;abcdef&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubeadm token list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(no tokens)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Bootstrap tokens expire after 24 hours unless a TTL was set. The token in the old notes was gone, so discovery failed. The fix was to print a new join command on a control plane node. Do not paste old join commands into runbooks: generate a fresh one each time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Mismatched kubeadm, kubelet and kubectl versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reusing an expired token or certificate key\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting the network plugin and wondering why nodes are NotReady\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using kubeadm reset and expecting a clean network configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building clusters with long flag lists instead of a file\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"prerequisites","text":"Prerequisites"},{"id":"phases-of-init","text":"Phases of init"},{"id":"a-configuration-file","text":"A configuration file"},{"id":"joining-nodes","text":"Joining nodes"},{"id":"reset-and-retry","text":"Reset and retry"},{"id":"incident-the-join-token-that-expired","text":"Incident: The join token that expired"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Creating a cluster with kubeadm","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/setup/production-environment/tools/kubeadm/create-cluster-kubeadm/"},{"t":"kubeadm configuration","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/config-api/kubeadm-config.v1beta4/"}],"text":"kubeadm sets up the pieces of a cluster in a defined order and leaves the nodes in a known state. Using a configuration file and understanding the phases means a cluster can be rebuilt the same way each time and a failed bootstrap can be repaired instead of restarted blindly. What you will learn Prerequisites on every node The main phases of kubeadm init A configuration file instead of flags Joining control plane and worker nodes Resetting and retrying safely Prerequisites Every node needs a container runtime with the CRI enabled, the kubelet, kubeadm and kubectl packages at matching versions, swap disabled unless configured for it, required kernel modules and sysctls for bridging and IP forwarding, unique host names, and open ports between nodes. Most bootstrap failures are a missed prerequisite, and kubeadm init runs preflight checks that name them. Phases of init kubeadm init runs phases in order: preflight, certificate generation, kubeconfig files, kubelet start, control plane static pod manifests, etcd static pod, wait for the control plane, upload configuration, mark the control plane node, bootstrap tokens and add-ons such as CoreDNS and kube-proxy. You can list and run a single phase with kubeadm init phase , which is useful for repairs, for example regenerating a certificate. A configuration file Flags are fine for a lab. For anything repeatable, use a configuration file with the InitConfiguration and ClusterConfiguration kinds. It records the Kubernetes version, the control plane endpoint, the pod and service subnets, certificate SANs and component flags. apiVersion: kubeadm.k8s.io/v1beta4 kind: ClusterConfiguration kubernetesVersion: v1.31.2 controlPlaneEndpoint: k8s-api.example.internal:6443 networking: podSubnet: 10.244 .0 .0 /16 serviceSubnet: 10.96 .0 .0 /12 apiServer: certSANs: - k8s-api.example.internal kubeadm init --config kubeadm.yaml --upload-certs Joining nodes Workers join with a token and the CA hash printed by init. Tokens expire after 24 hours by default, so create a new one with kubeadm token create --print-join-command . Additional control plane nodes join with the control plane flag and a certificate key, which is valid for two hours. After joining, install a network plugin if you have not, then check that all nodes are Ready. Reset and retry A failed init or join leaves files behind. kubeadm reset removes what kubeadm created on that node, but not CNI configuration, iptables rules or your kubeconfig. Clean those by hand before retrying. Read the preflight and phase output first, because the line that failed is usually clear. Bootstrapping with kubeadm: configuration and phases Flow in order: 1, Preflight to Certificates; 2, Certificates to Control plane; 3, Control plane to Add-ons; 4, Add-ons to Join nodes. Observability Security Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Preflight Observability What it does: Checks the node. If it fails or is weak: Swap or ports. Certificates Security What it does: Creates the CA and certs. If it fails or is weak: Wrong SANs. Control plane Service What it does: Static pod manifests. If it fails or is weak: Bad flags. Add-ons Service What it does: CoreDNS and kube-proxy. If it fails or is weak: No CNI yet. Join nodes Step What it does: Token and CA hash. If it fails or is weak: Expired token. Incident: The join token that expired Incident A new worker could not join a cluster built the week before. $ sudo kubeadm join k8s-api.example.internal:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1f2e3d error execution phase preflight: couldn't validate the identity of the API Server: could not find a JWS signature in the cluster-info ConfigMap for token ID \"abcdef\" $ kubeadm token list (no tokens) (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Bootstrap tokens expire after 24 hours unless a TTL was set. The token in the old notes was gone, so discover"},"k8s-lifecycle/certificates-pki-expiry-and-rotation":{"html":"\u003cp>A kubeadm cluster is held together by dozens of certificates. Most are valid for a year, and when they expire silently the API server and kubelets stop trusting each other. Knowing where they live and how to renew them prevents one of the most avoidable outages.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The certificate authorities and the leaf certificates they sign\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where files live and what each is for\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Checking and renewing expiry with kubeadm\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Kubelet client and serving certificate rotation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Recovering from expired certificates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-certificate-map\">The certificate map\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>kubeadm creates three authorities under \u003ccode>/etc/kubernetes/pki\u003c/code>: the cluster CA, the etcd CA and the front proxy CA. From them it signs the API server serving certificate, the API server client certificates for the kubelet and etcd, the etcd server, peer and health check certificates, and the service account signing key pair. The admin, controller manager and scheduler kubeconfig files embed client certificates too.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lifetimes\">Lifetimes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Authority certificates are valid for ten years. Leaf certificates are valid for one year. A cluster created and never upgraded will hit expiry about a year after creation. Each \u003ccode>kubeadm upgrade\u003c/code> renews leaf certificates, which is one reason regular upgrades are healthy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-and-renew\">Check and renew\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubeadm certs check-expiration\nkubeadm certs renew all\nsudo systemctl restart kubelet\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubeadm certs check-expiration\nkubeadm certs renew all\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl restart kubelet\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Renewing writes new files, but the control plane static pods must restart to load them. Moving the manifests out of the directory and back, or restarting the kubelet, achieves that. Repeat on every control plane node, and update your copy of \u003ccode>admin.conf\u003c/code> because it embeds a renewed client certificate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kubelet-certificates\">Kubelet certificates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each kubelet has a client certificate to talk to the API server. With \u003ccode>rotateCertificates\u003c/code> enabled it asks for a renewal through a CertificateSigningRequest before expiry, and the controller manager approves and signs it. Serving certificates can also be rotated with \u003ccode>serverTLSBootstrap\u003c/code>, but those requests need approval. Look for Pending CSRs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get csr\nkubectl certificate approve csr-abc12\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get csr\nkubectl certificate approve csr-abc12\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-they-have-already-expired\">When they have already expired\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>kubectl will fail with certificate has expired errors. On a control plane node, use the local admin kubeconfig and run \u003ccode>kubeadm certs renew all\u003c/code>. If the API server itself will not start, use the files and \u003ccode>crictl\u003c/code> as in the failure diagnosis lesson. Rotate the CA only with great care, because every client of the old CA must be updated.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"ca&gt;api;ca&gt;kc;ca&gt;etcd;kc&gt;kcfg\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Certificates: PKI, expiry and rotation\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ca\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cluster CA\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server cert\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kc\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Kubelet client cert\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"etcd\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">etcd certs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kcfg\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Kubeconfig certs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Cluster CA to API server cert; 2, Cluster CA to Kubelet client cert; 3, Cluster CA to etcd certs; 4, Kubelet client cert to Kubeconfig certs.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ca\">\u003cdt>Cluster CA \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Signs leaf certs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Key exposure.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server cert \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Serving identity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing SAN.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kc\">\u003cdt>Kubelet client cert \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Node identity.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rotation off.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"etcd\">\u003cdt>etcd certs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Peer and client.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Expired.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kcfg\">\u003cdt>Kubeconfig certs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Admin and components.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale copy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-expired-on-the-anniversary\">Incident: Expired on the anniversary\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>On the first anniversary of the cluster, kubectl and the controller manager stopped working at the same hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get nodes\nsudo kubeadm certs check-expiration | head -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Unable to connect to the server: x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid: current time 2026-11-14T09:00:00Z is after 2026-11-14T08:41:12Z\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm certs check-expiration | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME   CERTIFICATE AUTHORITY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">admin.conf                 Nov 14, 2026 08:41 UTC   &lt;invalid&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">apiserver                  Nov 14, 2026 08:41 UTC   &lt;invalid&gt;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cluster had never been upgraded, so no leaf certificate had been renewed in a year. Running kubeadm certs renew all on each control plane node, then restarting the control plane pods, restored access. A monitor on certificate expiry, with an alert at thirty days, would have turned an outage into a ticket.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Never checking certificate expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Renewing certificates but not restarting the static pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to update the admin kubeconfig copy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving kubelet CSRs Pending\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Renewing on one control plane node and not the others\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-certificate-map","text":"The certificate map"},{"id":"lifetimes","text":"Lifetimes"},{"id":"check-and-renew","text":"Check and renew"},{"id":"kubelet-certificates","text":"Kubelet certificates"},{"id":"when-they-have-already-expired","text":"When they have already expired"},{"id":"incident-expired-on-the-anniversary","text":"Incident: Expired on the anniversary"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Certificate management with kubeadm","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/kubeadm/kubeadm-certs/"},{"t":"PKI certificates and requirements","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/setup/best-practices/certificates/"}],"text":"A kubeadm cluster is held together by dozens of certificates. Most are valid for a year, and when they expire silently the API server and kubelets stop trusting each other. Knowing where they live and how to renew them prevents one of the most avoidable outages. What you will learn The certificate authorities and the leaf certificates they sign Where files live and what each is for Checking and renewing expiry with kubeadm Kubelet client and serving certificate rotation Recovering from expired certificates The certificate map kubeadm creates three authorities under /etc/kubernetes/pki : the cluster CA, the etcd CA and the front proxy CA. From them it signs the API server serving certificate, the API server client certificates for the kubelet and etcd, the etcd server, peer and health check certificates, and the service account signing key pair. The admin, controller manager and scheduler kubeconfig files embed client certificates too. Lifetimes Authority certificates are valid for ten years. Leaf certificates are valid for one year. A cluster created and never upgraded will hit expiry about a year after creation. Each kubeadm upgrade renews leaf certificates, which is one reason regular upgrades are healthy. Check and renew kubeadm certs check-expiration kubeadm certs renew all sudo systemctl restart kubelet Renewing writes new files, but the control plane static pods must restart to load them. Moving the manifests out of the directory and back, or restarting the kubelet, achieves that. Repeat on every control plane node, and update your copy of admin.conf because it embeds a renewed client certificate. Kubelet certificates Each kubelet has a client certificate to talk to the API server. With rotateCertificates enabled it asks for a renewal through a CertificateSigningRequest before expiry, and the controller manager approves and signs it. Serving certificates can also be rotated with serverTLSBootstrap , but those requests need approval. Look for Pending CSRs. kubectl get csr kubectl certificate approve csr-abc12 When they have already expired kubectl will fail with certificate has expired errors. On a control plane node, use the local admin kubeconfig and run kubeadm certs renew all . If the API server itself will not start, use the files and crictl as in the failure diagnosis lesson. Rotate the CA only with great care, because every client of the old CA must be updated. Certificates: PKI, expiry and rotation Flow in order: 1, Cluster CA to API server cert; 2, Cluster CA to Kubelet client cert; 3, Cluster CA to etcd certs; 4, Kubelet client cert to Kubeconfig certs. Security Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Cluster CA Security What it does: Signs leaf certs. If it fails or is weak: Key exposure. API server cert Security What it does: Serving identity. If it fails or is weak: Missing SAN. Kubelet client cert Security What it does: Node identity. If it fails or is weak: Rotation off. etcd certs Security What it does: Peer and client. If it fails or is weak: Expired. Kubeconfig certs Data store What it does: Admin and components. If it fails or is weak: Stale copy. Incident: Expired on the anniversary Incident On the first anniversary of the cluster, kubectl and the controller manager stopped working at the same hour. $ kubectl get nodes Unable to connect to the server: x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid: current time 2026-11-14T09:00:00Z is after 2026-11-14T08:41:12Z $ sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration | head -4 CERTIFICATE EXPIRES RESIDUAL TIME CERTIFICATE AUTHORITY admin.conf Nov 14, 2026 08:41 UTC \u003cinvalid> apiserver Nov 14, 2026 08:41 UTC \u003cinvalid> (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The cluster had never been upgraded, so no leaf certificate had been renewed in a year. Running kubeadm certs renew all on each control plane node, then restarting the control plane pods, restored access. A monitor o"},"k8s-lifecycle/version-skew-and-upgrade-strategy":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes releases a minor version about every four months, and each is supported for roughly a year. Staying inside the support window means upgrading regularly, and upgrading safely means respecting the skew rules between components.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Supported version skew between components\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why skipping minor versions is not allowed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The upgrade order\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pre-flight checks: deprecations, add-ons, backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>In-place and replace strategies\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"skew-rules\">Skew rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The API server is the reference. The controller manager and scheduler may be at most one minor version older than the API server. Kubelets may be up to three minor versions older in recent releases, never newer. kubectl may be one minor version newer or older. kube-proxy must match the kubelet on its node. Check the current policy for your version, since the numbers have changed over time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-minor-at-a-time\">One minor at a time\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A cluster must move through each minor version in turn. Upgrading from 1.29 to 1.31 means 1.29 to 1.30, then 1.30 to 1.31. Patch versions can be skipped within a minor. The reason is that API conversion and storage migrations are tested only between adjacent versions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-order\">The order\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Upgrade the control plane first, one node at a time, then the workers. On the first control plane node run \u003ccode>kubeadm upgrade plan\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>kubeadm upgrade apply\u003c/code>. On other control plane nodes run \u003ccode>kubeadm upgrade node\u003c/code>. For every node, drain it, upgrade the kubelet and kubectl packages, restart the kubelet and uncordon.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo kubeadm upgrade plan\nsudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.2\nkubectl drain cp2 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\nsudo systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl restart kubelet\nkubectl uncordon cp2\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm upgrade plan\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.2\nkubectl drain cp2 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> systemctl restart kubelet\nkubectl uncordon cp2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"before-you-start\">Before you start\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Read the release notes for deprecations and removed APIs. Run a tool such as kubent or pluto, or check apiserver_requested_deprecated_apis, to find manifests that use APIs about to be removed. Confirm that add-ons, the CNI plugin, ingress controller and operators support the target version. Take an etcd snapshot. Check that PodDisruptionBudgets will not block draining.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"in-place-or-replace\">In place or replace\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In place upgrades update the existing nodes, as above. The replace strategy builds new nodes at the new version, joins them, drains the old ones and deletes them. Replacement is cleaner for workers, especially with autoscaling groups or Cluster API, and gives a fast rollback because the old nodes still exist until you remove them. Control plane nodes with etcd data are usually upgraded in place or replaced one at a time with care.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"plan&gt;cp;cp&gt;wk;wk&gt;add;add&gt;ver\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Version skew and upgrade strategy\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plan\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Plan and backup\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cp\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Control plane\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wk\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Workers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"add\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Add-ons\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ver\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Verify\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Plan and backup to Control plane; 2, Control plane to Workers; 3, Workers to Add-ons; 4, Add-ons to Verify.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plan\">\u003cdt>Plan and backup \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Release notes and snapshot.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cp\">\u003cdt>Control plane \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One node at a time.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skew breach.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wk\">\u003cdt>Workers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Drain, upgrade, uncordon.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> PDB blocks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"add\">\u003cdt>Add-ons \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Match versions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Incompatible CNI.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ver\">\u003cdt>Verify \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Nodes and workloads.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No smoke test.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-removed-api-found-after-the-upgrade\">Incident: A removed API found after the upgrade\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After an upgrade a deployment pipeline began failing on every apply.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml\nkubectl api-versions | grep networking\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: resource mapping not found for name: &quot;web&quot; namespace: &quot;shop&quot; from &quot;ingress.yaml&quot;: no matches for kind &quot;Ingress&quot; in version &quot;extensions/v1beta1&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl api-versions | grep networking\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The manifests still used an API version that had been removed several releases earlier, and the upgrade finally crossed the removal. Scanning manifests and live objects for deprecated APIs before the upgrade would have shown it. Fix the manifests, then add the scan to the release checklist.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Skipping a minor version\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading workers before the control plane\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Draining without checking PodDisruptionBudgets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading without a snapshot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring add-on compatibility\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"skew-rules","text":"Skew rules"},{"id":"one-minor-at-a-time","text":"One minor at a time"},{"id":"the-order","text":"The order"},{"id":"before-you-start","text":"Before you start"},{"id":"in-place-or-replace","text":"In place or replace"},{"id":"incident-a-removed-api-found-after-the-upgrade","text":"Incident: A removed API found after the upgrade"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Upgrading kubeadm clusters","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/kubeadm/kubeadm-upgrade/"},{"t":"Version skew policy","u":"https://kubernetes.io/releases/version-skew-policy/"}],"text":"Kubernetes releases a minor version about every four months, and each is supported for roughly a year. Staying inside the support window means upgrading regularly, and upgrading safely means respecting the skew rules between components. What you will learn Supported version skew between components Why skipping minor versions is not allowed The upgrade order Pre-flight checks: deprecations, add-ons, backups In-place and replace strategies Skew rules The API server is the reference. The controller manager and scheduler may be at most one minor version older than the API server. Kubelets may be up to three minor versions older in recent releases, never newer. kubectl may be one minor version newer or older. kube-proxy must match the kubelet on its node. Check the current policy for your version, since the numbers have changed over time. One minor at a time A cluster must move through each minor version in turn. Upgrading from 1.29 to 1.31 means 1.29 to 1.30, then 1.30 to 1.31. Patch versions can be skipped within a minor. The reason is that API conversion and storage migrations are tested only between adjacent versions. The order Upgrade the control plane first, one node at a time, then the workers. On the first control plane node run kubeadm upgrade plan and kubeadm upgrade apply . On other control plane nodes run kubeadm upgrade node . For every node, drain it, upgrade the kubelet and kubectl packages, restart the kubelet and uncordon. sudo kubeadm upgrade plan sudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.2 kubectl drain cp2 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl restart kubelet kubectl uncordon cp2 Before you start Read the release notes for deprecations and removed APIs. Run a tool such as kubent or pluto, or check apiserver_requested_deprecated_apis, to find manifests that use APIs about to be removed. Confirm that add-ons, the CNI plugin, ingress controller and operators support the target version. Take an etcd snapshot. Check that PodDisruptionBudgets will not block draining. In place or replace In place upgrades update the existing nodes, as above. The replace strategy builds new nodes at the new version, joins them, drains the old ones and deletes them. Replacement is cleaner for workers, especially with autoscaling groups or Cluster API, and gives a fast rollback because the old nodes still exist until you remove them. Control plane nodes with etcd data are usually upgraded in place or replaced one at a time with care. Version skew and upgrade strategy Flow in order: 1, Plan and backup to Control plane; 2, Control plane to Workers; 3, Workers to Add-ons; 4, Add-ons to Verify. Observability Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Plan and backup Observability What it does: Release notes and snapshot. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Control plane Service What it does: One node at a time. If it fails or is weak: Skew breach. Workers Step What it does: Drain, upgrade, uncordon. If it fails or is weak: PDB blocks. Add-ons Service What it does: Match versions. If it fails or is weak: Incompatible CNI. Verify Observability What it does: Nodes and workloads. If it fails or is weak: No smoke test. Incident: A removed API found after the upgrade Incident After an upgrade a deployment pipeline began failing on every apply. $ kubectl apply -f ingress.yaml error: resource mapping not found for name: \"web\" namespace: \"shop\" from \"ingress.yaml\": no matches for kind \"Ingress\" in version \"extensions/v1beta1\" $ kubectl api-versions | grep networking networking.k8s.io/v1 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The manifests still used an API version that had been removed several releases earlier, and the upgrade finally crossed the removal. Scanning manifests and live objects for deprecated APIs before the upgrade would have shown it. Fix the manifests, then add the scan to the release checklist. C"},"k8s-lifecycle/node-lifecycle-joining-draining-and-replacing":{"html":"\u003cp>Nodes are cattle more than pets, but each one still needs a safe path in and out. Replacing a node well means workloads move gracefully, state is preserved and the cluster keeps its capacity throughout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Adding a node and what makes it Ready\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cordon, drain and uncordon\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why drains get stuck: PDBs, local storage, unmanaged pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Node problems and taints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replacing and removing a node cleanly\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"joining\">Joining\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A new node installs the runtime and kubelet, then joins with kubeadm or a cloud provider mechanism. It becomes Ready when the kubelet reports healthy and the CNI plugin has configured networking. Add labels for pool, zone and hardware so the scheduler and your selectors can use them, and taints for dedicated pools.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cordon-and-drain\">Cordon and drain\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cordon marks a node unschedulable, so no new pods land there, but existing pods keep running. Drain cordons and then evicts the pods, respecting PodDisruptionBudgets and graceful termination. Uncordon makes the node schedulable again.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl cordon worker-3\nkubectl drain worker-3 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --timeout=300s\nkubectl uncordon worker-3\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl cordon worker-3\nkubectl drain worker-3 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">timeout\u003c/span>=300s\nkubectl uncordon worker-3\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-a-drain-hangs\">Why a drain hangs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Drain waits for each eviction to succeed. A PodDisruptionBudget that allows zero disruptions blocks it. Pods not managed by a controller are refused unless you use force, because they will not be recreated. Pods with local storage need \u003ccode>--delete-emptydir-data\u003c/code>. DaemonSet pods are ignored with a flag. A pod that ignores SIGTERM will wait for its grace period before being killed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"unhealthy-nodes\">Unhealthy nodes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a node stops heartbeating, the node controller marks it NotReady and adds the \u003ccode>node.kubernetes.io/unreachable\u003c/code> taint, and after the default toleration of five minutes pods are evicted. Stateful pods with a ReadWriteOnce volume may need extra time to detach. Decide in advance whether to wait or to delete the node object after confirming the machine is really gone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replace-and-retire\">Replace and retire\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>To replace a node, add the new one first so capacity does not dip, drain the old one, check workloads are healthy, then delete the node object and the machine. If you delete the machine first, the node object remains until the controller notices.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl delete node worker-3\nkubectl get pods -A -o wide --field-selector spec.nodeName=worker-3\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl delete node worker-3\nkubectl get pods -A -o wide --field-selector spec.nodeName=worker-3\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"new&gt;cord;cord&gt;drain;drain&gt;ret\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Node lifecycle: joining, draining and replacing\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"new\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">New node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cord\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cordon\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"drain\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Drain\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ret\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Retire\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, New node to Cordon; 2, Cordon to Drain; 3, Drain to Retire.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"new\">\u003cdt>New node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Joins and becomes Ready.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No CNI.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cord\">\u003cdt>Cordon \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stops new pods.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Forgotten.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"drain\">\u003cdt>Drain \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Evicts pods.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> PDB blocks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ret\">\u003cdt>Retire \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Delete node and machine.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale node object.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-drain-that-never-finished\">Incident: The drain that never finished\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A node drain sat for forty minutes during a maintenance window.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl drain worker-3 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\nkubectl get pdb -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl drain worker-3 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">evicting pod shop/cart-7d9f\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error when evicting pods/&quot;cart-7d9f&quot; -n &quot;shop&quot; (will retry after 5s): Cannot evict pod as it would violate the pod's disruption budget.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pdb -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   MIN AVAILABLE   MAX UNAVAILABLE   ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cart   2               N/A               0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cart deployment had two replicas and a PodDisruptionBudget requiring two, so the budget allowed no disruptions and the drain retried forever. The fix was to scale to three replicas, which allowed one disruption, or to relax the budget for the window. Review PDBs against replica counts before planned maintenance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Draining without checking PodDisruptionBudgets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forcing eviction of pods that are not controller managed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting the machine before draining\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to uncordon after maintenance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the node object behind\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"joining","text":"Joining"},{"id":"cordon-and-drain","text":"Cordon and drain"},{"id":"why-a-drain-hangs","text":"Why a drain hangs"},{"id":"unhealthy-nodes","text":"Unhealthy nodes"},{"id":"replace-and-retire","text":"Replace and retire"},{"id":"incident-the-drain-that-never-finished","text":"Incident: The drain that never finished"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Safely drain a node","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/safely-drain-node/"},{"t":"Adding Linux worker nodes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/kubeadm/adding-linux-nodes/"}],"text":"Nodes are cattle more than pets, but each one still needs a safe path in and out. Replacing a node well means workloads move gracefully, state is preserved and the cluster keeps its capacity throughout. What you will learn Adding a node and what makes it Ready Cordon, drain and uncordon Why drains get stuck: PDBs, local storage, unmanaged pods Node problems and taints Replacing and removing a node cleanly Joining A new node installs the runtime and kubelet, then joins with kubeadm or a cloud provider mechanism. It becomes Ready when the kubelet reports healthy and the CNI plugin has configured networking. Add labels for pool, zone and hardware so the scheduler and your selectors can use them, and taints for dedicated pools. Cordon and drain Cordon marks a node unschedulable, so no new pods land there, but existing pods keep running. Drain cordons and then evicts the pods, respecting PodDisruptionBudgets and graceful termination. Uncordon makes the node schedulable again. kubectl cordon worker-3 kubectl drain worker-3 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data -- timeout =300s kubectl uncordon worker-3 Why a drain hangs Drain waits for each eviction to succeed. A PodDisruptionBudget that allows zero disruptions blocks it. Pods not managed by a controller are refused unless you use force, because they will not be recreated. Pods with local storage need --delete-emptydir-data . DaemonSet pods are ignored with a flag. A pod that ignores SIGTERM will wait for its grace period before being killed. Unhealthy nodes When a node stops heartbeating, the node controller marks it NotReady and adds the node.kubernetes.io/unreachable taint, and after the default toleration of five minutes pods are evicted. Stateful pods with a ReadWriteOnce volume may need extra time to detach. Decide in advance whether to wait or to delete the node object after confirming the machine is really gone. Replace and retire To replace a node, add the new one first so capacity does not dip, drain the old one, check workloads are healthy, then delete the node object and the machine. If you delete the machine first, the node object remains until the controller notices. kubectl delete node worker-3 kubectl get pods -A -o wide --field-selector spec.nodeName=worker-3 Node lifecycle: joining, draining and replacing Flow in order: 1, New node to Cordon; 2, Cordon to Drain; 3, Drain to Retire. Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. New node Service What it does: Joins and becomes Ready. If it fails or is weak: No CNI. Cordon Step What it does: Stops new pods. If it fails or is weak: Forgotten. Drain Step What it does: Evicts pods. If it fails or is weak: PDB blocks. Retire Step What it does: Delete node and machine. If it fails or is weak: Stale node object. Incident: The drain that never finished Incident A node drain sat for forty minutes during a maintenance window. $ kubectl drain worker-3 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data evicting pod shop/cart-7d9f error when evicting pods/\"cart-7d9f\" -n \"shop\" (will retry after 5s): Cannot evict pod as it would violate the pod's disruption budget. $ kubectl get pdb -n shop NAME MIN AVAILABLE MAX UNAVAILABLE ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS cart 2 N/A 0 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The cart deployment had two replicas and a PodDisruptionBudget requiring two, so the budget allowed no disruptions and the drain retried forever. The fix was to scale to three replicas, which allowed one disruption, or to relax the budget for the window. Review PDBs against replica counts before planned maintenance. Common mistakes Draining without checking PodDisruptionBudgets Forcing eviction of pods that are not controller managed Deleting the machine before draining Forgetting to uncordon after maintenance Leaving the node object behind"},"k8s-lifecycle/backup-and-disaster-recovery-for-the-cluster":{"html":"\u003cp>High availability keeps a cluster running through a node failure. It does not help when data is deleted, corrupted or when the whole site is lost. Those cases need backups, written recovery steps and rehearsals.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What state needs backing up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing RPO and RTO\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automating etcd snapshots and storing them safely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restoring one member and restoring a whole cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rehearsing and measuring recovery\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-back-up\">What to back up\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The essential item is etcd, which holds every object. Also keep \u003ccode>/etc/kubernetes/pki\u003c/code> and the kubeadm configuration, since certificates and the control plane endpoint matter for a rebuild. Application data in persistent volumes needs its own backups, with volume snapshots or an application-aware tool. Manifests should live in version control so that a fresh cluster can be rebuilt from them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rpo-and-rto\">RPO and RTO\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The recovery point objective is how much data you can afford to lose, which sets the snapshot frequency. The recovery time objective is how long the cluster can be down, which sets how automated and rehearsed the recovery must be. An hourly snapshot gives a one hour RPO. Write both numbers down, and measure them in a rehearsal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"automate-snapshots\">Automate snapshots\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run snapshots from a scheduled job on a control plane node, copy them off the node to separate storage, encrypt them because they contain Secrets, and keep several generations. Alert if a snapshot is missing or its size drops sharply.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /var/backups/etcd-$(date +%F-%H%M).db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\netcdutl snapshot status /var/backups/etcd-latest.db -w table\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /var/backups/etcd-$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">date\u003c/span> +%F-%H%M).db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\netcdutl snapshot status /var/backups/etcd-latest.db -w table\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restore-scenarios\">Restore scenarios\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Losing one member of a healthy cluster is routine: remove the dead member with \u003ccode>etcdctl member remove\u003c/code>, add a new one and let it sync. Losing quorum, or the whole cluster, means a restore from a snapshot. Restore the same snapshot on every member, with a new cluster token and the initial cluster list, or restore on one node and rebuild the others by joining them. After the restore, pods that were created after the snapshot will be missing and objects deleted since will have returned, so reconcile.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rehearse\">Rehearse\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A backup that was never restored is a hope. Schedule a drill in a scratch environment that restores a recent snapshot into a new cluster, checks that namespaces and workloads appear, and records how long it took. Update the runbook with what you learned.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"etcd&gt;snap;snap&gt;off;off&gt;rest\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Backup and disaster recovery for the cluster\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"etcd\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">etcd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"snap\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Snapshot job\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"off\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Off-node storage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rest\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Restore drill\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, etcd to Snapshot job; 2, Snapshot job to Off-node storage; 3, Off-node storage to Restore drill.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"etcd\">\u003cdt>etcd \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Live state.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Corruption.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"snap\">\u003cdt>Snapshot job \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scheduled saves.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Silent failure.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"off\">\u003cdt>Off-node storage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Encrypted copies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Same failure domain.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rest\">\u003cdt>Restore drill \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rebuild and time it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never rehearsed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-backups-that-stopped-in-march\">Incident: The backups that stopped in March\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During a recovery the newest snapshot was eight months old.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls -lt /var/backups/etcd-*.db | head -3\ncrontab -l | grep etcd\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -lt /var/backups/etcd-*.db | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw------- 1 root root 41943040 Mar  3 02:00 /var/backups/etcd-2026-03-03-0200.db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw------- 1 root root 41930752 Mar  2 02:00 /var/backups/etcd-2026-03-02-0200.db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>crontab -l | grep etcd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(no output)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A node rebuild had removed the cron entry, and nothing alerted on missing snapshots. The recovery used the March snapshot and lost months of changes, which declarative, repository-driven manifests and runbooks partly recovered. The team added an alert on snapshot age, moved the job into a controller, and scheduled a quarterly restore drill.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Never testing a restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping backups only on the control plane node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing snapshots unencrypted\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on missing backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that persistent volume data is separate\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-to-back-up","text":"What to back up"},{"id":"rpo-and-rto","text":"RPO and RTO"},{"id":"automate-snapshots","text":"Automate snapshots"},{"id":"restore-scenarios","text":"Restore scenarios"},{"id":"rehearse","text":"Rehearse"},{"id":"incident-the-backups-that-stopped-in-march","text":"Incident: The backups that stopped in March"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Operating etcd clusters for Kubernetes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/"},{"t":"etcd disaster recovery","u":"https://etcd.io/docs/latest/op-guide/recovery/"}],"text":"High availability keeps a cluster running through a node failure. It does not help when data is deleted, corrupted or when the whole site is lost. Those cases need backups, written recovery steps and rehearsals. What you will learn What state needs backing up Choosing RPO and RTO Automating etcd snapshots and storing them safely Restoring one member and restoring a whole cluster Rehearsing and measuring recovery What to back up The essential item is etcd, which holds every object. Also keep /etc/kubernetes/pki and the kubeadm configuration, since certificates and the control plane endpoint matter for a rebuild. Application data in persistent volumes needs its own backups, with volume snapshots or an application-aware tool. Manifests should live in version control so that a fresh cluster can be rebuilt from them. RPO and RTO The recovery point objective is how much data you can afford to lose, which sets the snapshot frequency. The recovery time objective is how long the cluster can be down, which sets how automated and rehearsed the recovery must be. An hourly snapshot gives a one hour RPO. Write both numbers down, and measure them in a rehearsal. Automate snapshots Run snapshots from a scheduled job on a control plane node, copy them off the node to separate storage, encrypt them because they contain Secrets, and keep several generations. Alert if a snapshot is missing or its size drops sharply. ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /var/backups/etcd-$( date +%F-%H%M).db --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key etcdutl snapshot status /var/backups/etcd-latest.db -w table Restore scenarios Losing one member of a healthy cluster is routine: remove the dead member with etcdctl member remove , add a new one and let it sync. Losing quorum, or the whole cluster, means a restore from a snapshot. Restore the same snapshot on every member, with a new cluster token and the initial cluster list, or restore on one node and rebuild the others by joining them. After the restore, pods that were created after the snapshot will be missing and objects deleted since will have returned, so reconcile. Rehearse A backup that was never restored is a hope. Schedule a drill in a scratch environment that restores a recent snapshot into a new cluster, checks that namespaces and workloads appear, and records how long it took. Update the runbook with what you learned. Backup and disaster recovery for the cluster Flow in order: 1, etcd to Snapshot job; 2, Snapshot job to Off-node storage; 3, Off-node storage to Restore drill. Data store Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. etcd Data store What it does: Live state. If it fails or is weak: Corruption. Snapshot job Step What it does: Scheduled saves. If it fails or is weak: Silent failure. Off-node storage Data store What it does: Encrypted copies. If it fails or is weak: Same failure domain. Restore drill Observability What it does: Rebuild and time it. If it fails or is weak: Never rehearsed. Incident: The backups that stopped in March Incident During a recovery the newest snapshot was eight months old. $ ls -lt /var/backups/etcd-*.db | head -3 -rw------- 1 root root 41943040 Mar 3 02:00 /var/backups/etcd-2026-03-03-0200.db -rw------- 1 root root 41930752 Mar 2 02:00 /var/backups/etcd-2026-03-02-0200.db $ crontab -l | grep etcd (no output) (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A node rebuild had removed the cron entry, and nothing alerted on missing snapshots. The recovery used the March snapshot and lost months of changes, which declarative, repository-driven manifests and runbooks partly recovered. The team added an alert on snapshot age, moved the job into a controller, and scheduled a quarterly restore drill. Common mistakes Never testing a restore Keeping backups only on th"},"k8s-lifecycle/cluster-api-and-declarative-cluster-management":{"html":"\u003cp>Scripts that build clusters tend to drift, and each cluster becomes a one-off. Cluster API takes the same approach Kubernetes uses for apps: describe the cluster you want as objects and let controllers make it so, including upgrades and replacement.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The idea of a management cluster and workload clusters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cluster, Machine, MachineDeployment and control plane objects\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How an upgrade becomes a version bump\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Providers and what they do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When it is worth the complexity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"management-and-workload-clusters\">Management and workload clusters\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cluster API runs in a management cluster, which can be a small dedicated cluster. It creates and manages workload clusters, on clouds, on virtual machines or on bare metal, through infrastructure providers. The management cluster holds the desired state of every workload cluster, so protecting it with backups matters.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-objects\">The objects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Cluster object ties the pieces together. A control plane object, commonly a KubeadmControlPlane, describes control plane machines and their kubeadm settings. A MachineDeployment is like a Deployment for worker nodes, with a template and a replica count, and a MachineSet and Machines beneath it. Infrastructure templates describe the cloud specific machine shape.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: cluster.x-k8s.io/v1beta1\nkind: MachineDeployment\nmetadata:\n  name: pool-a\nspec:\n  clusterName: prod\n  replicas: 5\n  template:\n    spec:\n      clusterName: prod\n      version: v1.31.2\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cluster.x-k8s.io/v1beta1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">MachineDeployment\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pool-a\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">clusterName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">prod\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicas:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">clusterName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">prod\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1.31.2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"upgrades-as-edits\">Upgrades as edits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>To upgrade workers, change the version in the MachineDeployment template. The controller creates new machines at that version and removes the old ones, a rolling replacement. For the control plane, change the version in the control plane object, and it replaces control plane machines one at a time, keeping etcd quorum. Scaling is changing a replica count.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"providers\">Providers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Providers do the cloud specific work: bootstrap providers create the kubeadm configuration for a machine, infrastructure providers create the virtual machine or server, and control plane providers manage control plane machines. Choose providers that match your platform and check their maturity.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"is-it-worth-it\">Is it worth it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For one small cluster, kubeadm and a runbook are simpler. With many clusters, frequent upgrades or a platform team offering clusters as a service, the declarative approach removes drift, makes replacement routine and gives an audit trail through version control. The trade is operating a management cluster and learning its failure modes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"mgmt&gt;cluster;cluster&gt;cp;cluster&gt;md;md&gt;infra\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Cluster API and declarative cluster management\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mgmt\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Management cluster\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cluster\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cluster object\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cp\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Control plane object\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"md\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">MachineDeployment\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"infra\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Infrastructure provider\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Management cluster to Cluster object; 2, Cluster object to Control plane object; 3, Cluster object to MachineDeployment; 4, MachineDeployment to Infrastructure provider.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mgmt\">\u003cdt>Management cluster \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds desired state.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unbacked.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cluster\">\u003cdt>Cluster object \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Describes a cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong version.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cp\">\u003cdt>Control plane object \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Control plane machines.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Quorum risk.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"md\">\u003cdt>MachineDeployment \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Worker machines.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bad template.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"infra\">\u003cdt>Infrastructure provider \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates machines.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Quota.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-template-change-that-replaced-every-worker\">Incident: A template change that replaced every worker\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A small edit to a machine template rolled all worker nodes at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get machines -n prod\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get machines -n prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            PHASE          VERSION\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pool-a-abc12    Provisioning   v1.31.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pool-a-def34    Provisioning   v1.31.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pool-a-ghi56    Deleting       v1.30.5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A change to a machine template triggers a rolling replacement, and the rollout settings allowed too many machines to be replaced at once for a workload with strict capacity needs. The fix was to set maxUnavailable to one and add PodDisruptionBudgets. Declarative tools make big changes easy, so rollout limits matter more, not less.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Not backing up the management cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating machines as pets and editing them by hand\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting rollout limits too wide\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing immature providers without testing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using it for one small cluster where it adds little\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"management-and-workload-clusters","text":"Management and workload clusters"},{"id":"the-objects","text":"The objects"},{"id":"upgrades-as-edits","text":"Upgrades as edits"},{"id":"providers","text":"Providers"},{"id":"is-it-worth-it","text":"Is it worth it"},{"id":"incident-a-template-change-that-replaced-every-worker","text":"Incident: A template change that replaced every worker"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Cluster API repository","u":"https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/cluster-api"},{"t":"Kubernetes cluster architecture","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/"}],"text":"Scripts that build clusters tend to drift, and each cluster becomes a one-off. Cluster API takes the same approach Kubernetes uses for apps: describe the cluster you want as objects and let controllers make it so, including upgrades and replacement. What you will learn The idea of a management cluster and workload clusters Cluster, Machine, MachineDeployment and control plane objects How an upgrade becomes a version bump Providers and what they do When it is worth the complexity Management and workload clusters Cluster API runs in a management cluster, which can be a small dedicated cluster. It creates and manages workload clusters, on clouds, on virtual machines or on bare metal, through infrastructure providers. The management cluster holds the desired state of every workload cluster, so protecting it with backups matters. The objects A Cluster object ties the pieces together. A control plane object, commonly a KubeadmControlPlane, describes control plane machines and their kubeadm settings. A MachineDeployment is like a Deployment for worker nodes, with a template and a replica count, and a MachineSet and Machines beneath it. Infrastructure templates describe the cloud specific machine shape. apiVersion: cluster.x-k8s.io/v1beta1 kind: MachineDeployment metadata: name: pool-a spec: clusterName: prod replicas: 5 template: spec: clusterName: prod version: v1.31.2 Upgrades as edits To upgrade workers, change the version in the MachineDeployment template. The controller creates new machines at that version and removes the old ones, a rolling replacement. For the control plane, change the version in the control plane object, and it replaces control plane machines one at a time, keeping etcd quorum. Scaling is changing a replica count. Providers Providers do the cloud specific work: bootstrap providers create the kubeadm configuration for a machine, infrastructure providers create the virtual machine or server, and control plane providers manage control plane machines. Choose providers that match your platform and check their maturity. Is it worth it For one small cluster, kubeadm and a runbook are simpler. With many clusters, frequent upgrades or a platform team offering clusters as a service, the declarative approach removes drift, makes replacement routine and gives an audit trail through version control. The trade is operating a management cluster and learning its failure modes. Cluster API and declarative cluster management Flow in order: 1, Management cluster to Cluster object; 2, Cluster object to Control plane object; 3, Cluster object to MachineDeployment; 4, MachineDeployment to Infrastructure provider. Service Data store Step Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Management cluster Service What it does: Holds desired state. If it fails or is weak: Unbacked. Cluster object Data store What it does: Describes a cluster. If it fails or is weak: Wrong version. Control plane object Service What it does: Control plane machines. If it fails or is weak: Quorum risk. MachineDeployment Step What it does: Worker machines. If it fails or is weak: Bad template. Infrastructure provider Outside party What it does: Creates machines. If it fails or is weak: Quota. Incident: A template change that replaced every worker Incident A small edit to a machine template rolled all worker nodes at once. $ kubectl get machines -n prod NAME PHASE VERSION pool-a-abc12 Provisioning v1.31.2 pool-a-def34 Provisioning v1.31.2 pool-a-ghi56 Deleting v1.30.5 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A change to a machine template triggers a rolling replacement, and the rollout settings allowed too many machines to be replaced at once for a workload with strict capacity needs. The fix was to set maxUnavailable to one and add PodDisruptionBudgets. Declarative tools make big changes easy, so rollout limits matter more, not less. Common mistakes Not backing u"},"k8s-lifecycle/capstone-plan-and-rehearse-an-upgrade-and-a-recovery":{"html":"\u003cp>The best sign of a mature cluster is that the scary tasks are boring. This capstone turns what you have learned into two documents you can hand to a colleague, an upgrade plan and a recovery runbook, and a rehearsal that proves they work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What an upgrade plan must contain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What a recovery runbook must contain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to rehearse safely and measure the result\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A readiness checklist you can reuse\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-upgrade-plan\">The upgrade plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write the plan as numbered steps with an owner, an expected duration and a check after each step. Include the current and target versions and the version skew check. List preparation tasks: release notes, deprecated API scan, add-on compatibility, an etcd snapshot, PodDisruptionBudget review and a maintenance window. Describe the order, control plane first, then workers in batches that keep capacity. Define a stop point after the first control plane node where you can still roll back.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rollback-thinking\">Rollback thinking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Control plane upgrades are not easily reversible once storage schema migrations have run, so the safe rollback is a restore from the snapshot taken before. Worker upgrades are reversible by replacing nodes with the previous version. Say clearly what you will do at each stop point if a check fails, and who decides.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-recovery-runbook\">The recovery runbook\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cover the cases you can name: one control plane node lost, etcd quorum lost, all control plane nodes lost, expired certificates and a deleted namespace. For each give symptoms, the first checks, the exact commands with placeholders you fill in at the time, expected output, and how to verify the result. Keep it where you can read it when the cluster is down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rehearse-and-measure\">Rehearse and measure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Build a scratch cluster that mirrors production in shape, run the upgrade plan end to end, then break it on purpose and use the runbook to recover. Time each step, note surprises and fix the documents. Repeat each quarter, and after major changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-readiness-check-in-code\">A readiness check in code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"checks = {\n    &quot;etcd snapshot taken and verified&quot;: True,\n    &quot;deprecated API scan clean&quot;: True,\n    &quot;add-ons support target version&quot;: True,\n    &quot;PDBs allow one disruption&quot;: False,\n    &quot;rollback owner named&quot;: True,\n}\nblocked = [k for k, ok in checks.items() if not ok]\nprint(&quot;ready&quot; if not blocked else &quot;blocked by: &quot; + &quot;, &quot;.join(blocked))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">checks = {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;etcd snapshot taken and verified&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deprecated API scan clean&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;add-ons support target version&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;PDBs allow one disruption&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rollback owner named&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>,\n}\nblocked = [k \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> k, ok \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> checks.items() \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> ok]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ready&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> blocked \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;blocked by: &quot;\u003c/span> + \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;, &quot;\u003c/span>.join(blocked))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"plan&gt;run;run&gt;drill;drill&gt;meas\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Capstone: plan and rehearse an upgrade and a recovery\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plan\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Upgrade plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Runbook\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"drill\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rehearsal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"meas\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Measured times\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Upgrade plan to Runbook; 2, Runbook to Rehearsal; 3, Rehearsal to Measured times.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plan\">\u003cdt>Upgrade plan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Steps, checks, stop points.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague steps.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>Runbook \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Recovery commands.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Out of date.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"drill\">\u003cdt>Rehearsal \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Scratch cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not like prod.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"meas\">\u003cdt>Measured times \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> RTO evidence.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No timing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-runbook-that-lived-on-the-broken-cluster\">Incident: The runbook that lived on the broken cluster\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During an outage the only copy of the runbook was in a wiki hosted on the failed cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -sI https://wiki.corp.example/k8s-recovery | head -1\nls ~/runbooks\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sI https://wiki.corp.example/k8s-recovery | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">curl: (7) Failed to connect to wiki.corp.example port 443: Connection refused\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> ~/runbooks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(no such directory)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The documentation platform ran on the same cluster that had failed, so nobody could read the steps. The team moved the runbooks to version control, copied them to a read only share and a printed binder for the on-call, and added a check that the runbook location does not depend on the cluster. Test that you can reach your recovery documents with the cluster down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Plans without stop points or owners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Runbooks stored on the system they recover\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rehearsing only the happy path\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never timing the steps\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not updating documents after a drill\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-upgrade-plan","text":"The upgrade plan"},{"id":"rollback-thinking","text":"Rollback thinking"},{"id":"the-recovery-runbook","text":"The recovery runbook"},{"id":"rehearse-and-measure","text":"Rehearse and measure"},{"id":"a-readiness-check-in-code","text":"A readiness check in code"},{"id":"incident-the-runbook-that-lived-on-the-broken-cluster","text":"Incident: The runbook that lived on the broken cluster"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Upgrading kubeadm clusters","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/kubeadm/kubeadm-upgrade/"},{"t":"Operating etcd clusters for Kubernetes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/"}],"text":"The best sign of a mature cluster is that the scary tasks are boring. This capstone turns what you have learned into two documents you can hand to a colleague, an upgrade plan and a recovery runbook, and a rehearsal that proves they work. What you will learn What an upgrade plan must contain What a recovery runbook must contain How to rehearse safely and measure the result A readiness checklist you can reuse The upgrade plan Write the plan as numbered steps with an owner, an expected duration and a check after each step. Include the current and target versions and the version skew check. List preparation tasks: release notes, deprecated API scan, add-on compatibility, an etcd snapshot, PodDisruptionBudget review and a maintenance window. Describe the order, control plane first, then workers in batches that keep capacity. Define a stop point after the first control plane node where you can still roll back. Rollback thinking Control plane upgrades are not easily reversible once storage schema migrations have run, so the safe rollback is a restore from the snapshot taken before. Worker upgrades are reversible by replacing nodes with the previous version. Say clearly what you will do at each stop point if a check fails, and who decides. The recovery runbook Cover the cases you can name: one control plane node lost, etcd quorum lost, all control plane nodes lost, expired certificates and a deleted namespace. For each give symptoms, the first checks, the exact commands with placeholders you fill in at the time, expected output, and how to verify the result. Keep it where you can read it when the cluster is down. Rehearse and measure Build a scratch cluster that mirrors production in shape, run the upgrade plan end to end, then break it on purpose and use the runbook to recover. Time each step, note surprises and fix the documents. Repeat each quarter, and after major changes. A readiness check in code checks = { \"etcd snapshot taken and verified\" : True , \"deprecated API scan clean\" : True , \"add-ons support target version\" : True , \"PDBs allow one disruption\" : False , \"rollback owner named\" : True , } blocked = [k for k, ok in checks.items() if not ok] print ( \"ready\" if not blocked else \"blocked by: \" + \", \" .join(blocked)) Capstone: plan and rehearse an upgrade and a recovery Flow in order: 1, Upgrade plan to Runbook; 2, Runbook to Rehearsal; 3, Rehearsal to Measured times. Data store Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Upgrade plan Data store What it does: Steps, checks, stop points. If it fails or is weak: Vague steps. Runbook Data store What it does: Recovery commands. If it fails or is weak: Out of date. Rehearsal Step What it does: Scratch cluster. If it fails or is weak: Not like prod. Measured times Observability What it does: RTO evidence. If it fails or is weak: No timing. Incident: The runbook that lived on the broken cluster Incident During an outage the only copy of the runbook was in a wiki hosted on the failed cluster. $ curl -sI https://wiki.corp.example/k8s-recovery | head -1 curl: (7) Failed to connect to wiki.corp.example port 443: Connection refused $ ls ~/runbooks (no such directory) (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The documentation platform ran on the same cluster that had failed, so nobody could read the steps. The team moved the runbooks to version control, copied them to a read only share and a printed binder for the on-call, and added a check that the runbook location does not depend on the cluster. Test that you can reach your recovery documents with the cluster down. Common mistakes Plans without stop points or owners Runbooks stored on the system they recover Rehearsing only the happy path Never timing the steps Not updating documents after a drill"}});